<h2>The situation</h2>
<p>A Japanese online securities firm ran its trading and account platforms on AWS. Over the years it had connected a long list of suppliers: a data platform that read market and customer data through a cross-account role, an observability vendor with read access to logs and metrics, a managed service provider that ran remote management agents on office devices, and SaaS tools connected to the firm's email and file storage through OAuth grants. Each connection had been approved by the team that needed it, at the time it was needed. Nobody had a complete list.</p>
<p>The FSA's cybersecurity guidelines for the financial sector, in effect since October 2024, treat third-party risk management as a core area, including outsourcing and cloud services<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. The firm's supplier assessments were thorough on paper: questionnaires, certifications and contract clauses. What they did not show was what each supplier could technically do inside the firm's systems today.</p>
<p><strong>Why now.</strong> In early November 2026, Wiz reported that an attacker had used credentials tied to Qubole's AWS account to assume integration roles that customers had created to trust Qubole, then collected secrets and data from those customers' accounts<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Days later, N-able took its hosted N-central remote management platform offline after a researcher published alleged zero-days<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. Both showed the same thing: a supplier's access is only as safe as the supplier.</p>

<h2>What had to be true</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Known</strong>: every supplier's technical access in one ledger.</li>
<li><strong>Owned</strong>: an internal owner and a business reason for each.</li>
<li><strong>Narrow</strong>: each supplier can do only what its service needs.</li>
<li><strong>Watched</strong>: unusual supplier activity raises an alert.</li>
<li><strong>Revocable</strong>: any supplier's access can be cut quickly, without guesswork.</li>
<li><strong>Evidenced</strong>: supervisors and auditors can see all of the above.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Architecture</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-stack">
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Suppliers</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Data platform<em>cross-account role</em></span><span>Observability<em>read-only role</em></span><span>Managed service provider<em>device agents</em></span><span>SaaS tools<em>OAuth grants</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Access</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span class="hl">Narrow roles<em>specific actions and resources</em></span><span>Unique external IDs<em>per relationship</em></span><span>Just-in-time access<em>for remote management</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Guardrails</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Service control policies<em>what no vendor may ever do</em></span><span class="hl">Data perimeter<em>expected networks only</em></span><span>Secrets out of reach<em>not in vendor-readable places</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Detection</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>CloudTrail<em>every vendor action</em></span><span class="hl">Behaviour alerts<em>new source, time or action</em></span></div></div>
<div class="dgm-tier"><small>Governance</small><div class="dgm-nodes"><span>Vendor access ledger</span><span>Revocation runbooks</span><span>Contract terms</span></div></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Each layer assumes the one above it might fail: narrow roles, then guardrails, then detection, then a way to cut access.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Every cross-account role was rebuilt following AWS's guidance for third-party access: a trust policy naming the vendor's account, a unique external ID for the relationship and permissions limited to the specific actions and resources the service needed<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. Service control policies set limits no supplier role could exceed, such as never reading the secrets manager or changing identity settings. Where a vendor could commit to fixed source networks, resource policies only accepted vendor access from those networks. The model is the one Kubernetes teaches for role-based access: a narrowly defined role, bound explicitly to named identities<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/japan-securities-third-party-cloud-access/narrow-role-binding.webp" alt="Diagram of a pod-reader role allowing only get and list on pods, bound through a role binding to a user, a group and a service account" width="624" height="271" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>A narrow role bound to named identities: the same shape the firm used for every supplier's access, whatever the platform. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://kubernetes.io/blog/2018/07/18/11-ways-not-to-get-hacked/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kubernetes blog, Andrew Martin</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>

<h2>The data flow, end to end</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>Request</b><span>A supplier's system asks to assume its role in the firm's account</span></li>
<li><b>Check trust</b><span>The trust policy checks the vendor account and external ID</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Limit</b><span>The role, guardrails and data perimeter decide what is allowed</span></li>
<li><b>Act</b><span>The supplier performs only the actions its service needs</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Watch</b><span>CloudTrail records each action; unusual patterns raise an alert</span></li>
<li><b>Cut</b><span>If needed, the runbook disables the trust and revokes sessions</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>Trust is checked at the door, limited inside and watched throughout.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>The hard part: when the supplier itself is compromised</h2>
<p><strong>External IDs are not enough.</strong> An external ID protects against the confused deputy problem, where one customer tricks a vendor into acting on someone else's account<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. It does not help when the vendor's own systems are compromised; in the Qubole case, the attacker's requests were accepted by customers' roles<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. So the firm assumed every supplier could one day be the attacker, and designed so that a compromised supplier could reach only a narrow slice of data, could not read secrets and would trigger alerts by behaving differently.</p>
<p><strong>Detecting "normal but wrong".</strong> A compromised supplier uses valid credentials, so authentication alerts stay quiet. The firm built a simple baseline per supplier: usual source networks, usual hours, usual API actions and usual data volumes. Departures from that baseline, such as a new network, listing every bucket or reading parameters it had never read, raised alerts to the security team and the supplier's internal owner.</p>
<p><strong>Remote management tools.</strong> The managed service provider's agents could run commands on every office device, so they received the strictest treatment: access only to office devices, never to trading systems; just-in-time sessions approved per task; and a tested way to stop the agents across the fleet if the provider was compromised. See the <a href="/case-studies/uk-msp-cyber-resilience-bill-readiness/">MSP cyber resilience case study</a>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>Assume every supplier will one day be breached. Design so that day costs you a narrow slice of data and an alert, not your whole estate.</p></div>

<h2>How it ran</h2>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-lanes">
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Security</small><ol><li>Access inventory</li><li class="hl">Guardrails and alerts</li><li>Revocation drills</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Platform</small><ol><li>Rebuild vendor roles</li><li class="hl">Data perimeter</li><li>Move secrets out of reach</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Vendor owners</small><ol><li>Confirm business need</li><li class="hl">Define normal behaviour</li><li>Review quarterly</li></ol></div>
<div class="dgm-lane"><small>Risk, legal and procurement</small><ol><li>Map to FSA guidelines</li><li>Notification clauses</li><li class="hl">Evidence for supervisors</li></ol></div>
</div>
<figcaption>Security and platform built the controls; the people who own each supplier relationship kept them accurate.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure class="dgm">
<div class="dgm-phases">
<div><small>Phase 1</small><b>Find</b><ul><li>Roles trusting outside accounts</li><li>OAuth grants and agents</li><li>Owners and reasons</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 2</small><b>Narrow</b><ul><li>Rebuild roles</li><li>Remove unused access</li><li>Guardrails</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 3</small><b>Watch</b><ul><li>Behaviour baselines</li><li>Alerts to owners</li><li>Data perimeter</li></ul></div>
<div><small>Phase 4</small><b>Rehearse</b><ul><li>Revoke-a-vendor drills</li><li>Contract updates</li><li>Quarterly reviews</li></ul></div>
</div>
<figcaption>The first phase alone removed access nobody could justify.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>Key decisions and trade-offs</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Alternative</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Technical ledger of access, not only questionnaires</td><td>Rely on supplier assessments</td><td>Shows what suppliers can actually do today</td></tr>
<tr><td>Assume supplier compromise</td><td>Trust certified suppliers</td><td>Valid credentials are exactly what attackers use</td></tr>
<tr><td>Guardrails no vendor role can exceed</td><td>Careful role design only</td><td>One mistake in a role does not expose everything</td></tr>
<tr><td>Behaviour alerts per supplier</td><td>Generic anomaly detection</td><td>Small baselines are cheap and catch "normal but wrong"</td></tr>
<tr><td>Just-in-time remote management</td><td>Always-on agent access</td><td>Limits what a compromised provider can do at any moment</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rehearsed per-vendor revocation</td><td>Revoke when needed</td><td>Cutting access in a crisis needs a known, safe procedure</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>What I'd do again, and differently</h2>
<p>Again: start with the inventory of roles trusting outside accounts. It found integrations for services the firm had stopped using years earlier, still active. Differently: involve procurement from the start, so new suppliers are onboarded into the ledger rather than discovered later. Related: <a href="/insights/tech-news-2026-11-10/">the 10 November tech news</a>, the <a href="/case-studies/south-africa-fintech-machine-agent-identity/">machine and agent identity case study</a>, <a href="/insights/securing-mcp-servers-checklist/">securing MCP servers</a> and <a href="/regions/">where I work</a>.</p>
<p>If you cannot list every supplier that can reach your cloud, or cut one off quickly, see my <a href="/fractional-cto/">fractional CTO work</a>, or <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
